afgq|v|of Vie ; a. & n. from Vie. W () the twenty-third letter of the English alphabet, is usually a consonant, but sometimes it is a vowel, forming the second element of certain diphthongs, as in few, how. It takes its written form and its name from the repetition of a V, this being the original form of the Roman capital letter which we call U. Etymologically it is most related to v and u. See V, and U. Some of the uneducated classes in England, especially in London, confuse w and v, substituting the one for the other, as weal for veal, and veal for weal; wine for vine, and vine for wine, etc. See Guide to Pronunciation, // 266-268.	s:35ah,127,13;1932,83,8;7fel,65,11;9mgi,55,7;70j5,41,18;3vm1,38,3;7nda,23,4~70j5,41,18;35ah,127,13;7fel,65,11;1932,83,8;9mgi,55,7;7nda,23,4;3vm1,38,3	d:axy,15,13;934o,13,5~axy,15,13;934o,13,5
